[
  {
    "id" : "case-sensitive-variables",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Consistent Casing for Variable Names",
    "description" : "Ensures that variable names use consistent casing when referenced multiple times in T-SQL code.",
    "explanation" : "# Enforces common casing for variable references\n\n## Rule Details\n\nVariable names must use common casing when referenced multiple times.\n\nExamples of **incorrect** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\nDECLARE @ProductID INT = 25;\nSELECT @PRODUCTID;\nGO\n```\n\nExamples of **correct** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\nDECLARE @ProductID INT = 25;\nSELECT @ProductID;\nGO\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "conditional-begin-end",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce BEGIN and END in Conditional Logic Blocks",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of BEGIN and END statements to define a block of code within conditional statements in T-SQL.",
    "explanation" : "# Enforce use of BEGIN and END symbols inside condition statements\n\n## Rule Details\n\nUse BEGIN and END to bind conditional statments as a single block of code.\n\nExamples of **incorrect** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\n  IF (@parm = 1)\n    SELECT @output = ‘foo’\n```\n\nExamples of **correct** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\n  IF (@parm = 1)\n  BEGIN\n    SELECT @output = ‘foo’\n  END\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "count-star",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Disallow COUNT(*) Usage",
    "description" : "Disallows the use of COUNT(*) in SQL queries and suggests using COUNT(1) or COUNT(<PrimaryKey>) instead.",
    "explanation" : "# Disallows use of count-star\n\n## Rule Details\n\nUse of `COUNT(*)` is disallowed. Suggested alternatives are `COUNT(1)` or `COUNT(<PK>)`.\n\nExamples of **incorrect** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\nSELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM Production.Product;\nGO\n```\n\nExamples of **correct** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\nSELECT COUNT(1)\nFROM Production.Product;\nGO\n```\n\n```tsql\nSELECT COUNT([ProductID])\nFROM Production.Product;\nGO\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "cross-database-transaction",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Cross-Database Transactions for Inserts or Updates",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages inserts or updates that span multiple databases within a single transaction to prevent data corruption.",
    "explanation" : "# Discourages inserts or updates that create transactions in more than one database\n\n## Rule Details\n\nCross database inserts or updates enclosed in a transaction can lead to data corruption\n\nExamples of **incorrect** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\n  BEGIN TRANSACTION;\n    UPDATE DB1.dbo.Table1 SET Value = 1;\n    UPDATE DB2.dbo.Table2 SET Value = 1;\n  COMMIT TRANSACTION;\n```\n\nExamples of **correct** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\n  BEGIN TRANSACTION;\n    UPDATE DB1.dbo.Table1 SET Value = 1;\n  COMMIT TRANSACTION;\n  \n  BEGIN TRANSACTION;\n    UPDATE DB2.dbo.Table2 SET Value = 1;\n  COMMIT TRANSACTION\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "data-compression",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Data Compression on Tables",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of data compression options during table creation in T-SQL to optimize storage and performance.",
    "explanation" : "# Requires use of compression option during table creation\n\n## Rule Details\n\nData compression can reduce database size and can help improve performance of I/O intensive workloads\n\nExamples of **incorrect** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\n  CREATE TABLE MyTable\n    (ID INT,\n    Name nvarchar(50))\n```\n\nExamples of **correct** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\n  CREATE TABLE MyTable\n    (ID INT,\n    Name nvarchar(50))\n  WITH (DATA_COMPRESSION = ROW);\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "data-type-length",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Data Type Length Specification",
    "description" : "Ensures that variable-length data types in SQL declarations specify their length explicitly.",
    "explanation" : "# Requires use of length when declaring data types with variable length\n\n## Rule Details\n\nUse of length when specifying data types can help with database size and improve I/O\n\nExamples of **incorrect** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\n  CREATE TABLE MyTable\n    (ID INT,\n     Name nvarchar);\n```\n\nExamples of **correct** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\n  CREATE TABLE MyTable\n  (ID INT,\n  Name nvarchar(50))\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "delete-where",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce WHERE Clause in DELETE Statements",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that DELETE statements include a WHERE clause to specify which rows to delete.",
    "explanation" : "# Enforce use of WHERE condition when using DELETE\n\n## Rule Details\n\nThis rule enforces the use of a WHERE condition in DELETE statements.\n\nExamples of **incorrect** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\nDELETE\nFROM dbo.MyTable;\n```\n\nExamples of **correct** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\nDELETE\nFROM dbo.MyTable\nWHERE CreatedOn = GETDATE();\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "disallow-cursors",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Disallow Use of CURSOR Statements",
    "description" : "This rule disallows the use of CURSOR statements in T-SQL code to prevent row-based operations.",
    "explanation" : "# Disallows use of cursors\n\n## Rule Details\n\nUse of cursors introduces a pattern of row based operations, which less preferred to better performing set based operations\n\nExamples of **incorrect** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\nDECLARE @id int\nDECLARE cursorT CURSOR\nFOR\nSELECT ProductId\nFROM AdventureWorks2012.Production.Product\nWHERE DaysToManufacture <= 1\n\nOPEN cursorT\nFETCH NEXT FROM cursorT INTO @id\nWHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0\nBEGIN\n          SELECT * FROM Production.ProductInventory\n          WHERE ProductID=@id\n          FETCH NEXT FROM cursorT INTO @id\nEND\nCLOSE cursorT\nDEALLOCATE cursorT\n```\n\nExamples of **correct** code for this rule:\n\n```tsql\n-- Alternative to cursor statement, using join\n\nSET STATISTICS TIME ON\nSELECT * FROM Production.ProductInventory as pinv\nINNER JOIN Production.Product as pp\nON pinv.ProductID=pp.ProductID\nWHERE pp.DaysToManufacture <= 1\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "duplicate-empty-line",
